Log the exception class in handle_exception().

Every error handler funnels through handle_exception(), which logged only
str(ex). An AuthenticationError (credentials dead, needs action) and a
RateLimitingError (back off, resolves itself) were indistinguishable in the
log. That ambiguity cost two months of lost data, twice.

Not upstream.

--- a/monitor.py
+++ b/monitor.py
@@ -619,7 +619,9 @@ def handle_exception(ex: Exception, retries: int, stacktrace=False) -> tuple[int
     - 9999: "Undefined Error - Response timeout"
     """
     exception_str = str(ex)
-    error_string = f"Exception: {exception_str}"
+    # Log the class too: every handler funnels into here, so without it an
+    # AuthenticationError and a RateLimitingError look identical in the log.
+    error_string = f"Exception: {type(ex).__name__}: {exception_str}"
     logging.warning(error_string)
     if stacktrace and "Service Temporary Unavailable" not in exception_str:
         traceback.print_exc()
